        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: It Depends on the Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            Does bleach kill mold? Chlorine bleach is a strong oxidiser, and when it reaches mold on a smooth, sealed surface it kills it. That is not in dispute, and it is why bathroom sprays built on sodium hypochlorite, such as Clorox Plus Tilex Mold &amp; Mildew Remover, carry a kill claim on the label. Clorox says the product kills 99.9 percent of mold and mildew, and on glazed tile, glass and fiberglass that is a fair description of what happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trouble is that most mold in a house is not growing on glazed tile. It is on the back of drywall, in the grain of a joist, along a wooden window sill, in the paper of a basement wall or deep in an old grout line. Those materials are porous. Mold sends its root-like filaments, called hyphae, into them, and a liquid sprayed on the face of the material does not reliably follow. What bleach does very well on those surfaces is remove colour, so the dark patch fades and the job looks done.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the heart of the bleach question. Bleach removes the stain, and the stain is the only part of the mold most people can see. When the black spots vanish in thirty seconds it is natural to assume the mold is dead, but the visible stain and the living colony are not the same thing. If the colony is inside the material and the moisture that fed it is still there, it comes back, often in the same outline, within weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer has three parts. Yes, bleach kills mold on hard, non-porous surfaces, and it is a reasonable choice for shower grout, tile and tubs. No, it is not a dependable mold killer for wood, drywall, ceiling tiles, carpet or concrete, and its own makers say not to use it on several of those. And whatever you use, the EPA&#39;s guidance is that the real job is removing the mold and fixing the moisture, not sterilising the surface.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bleach for tile, glass and tubs. Not for wood, drywall or anything that soaks up water.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If water soaks into it, bleach will not reach the mold inside it. Use a registered non-bleach fungicide there, and fix the moisture source either way.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="where-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Bleach Genuinely Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bleach earns its reputation in the bathroom. Shower mildew grows on the surface of glazed ceramic tile, glass doors, acrylic and fiberglass surrounds and the silicone at the edge of the tub. None of those materials lets the mold get inside, so the whole colony is exposed to whatever you spray on it. A bleach-based spray reaches all of it, kills it and removes the black pigment in one step, which is why a dirty shower can look new in a minute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clorox is unusually specific about where its Tilex spray belongs. The product page lists grout, glazed ceramic tile, tubs, fiberglass, glass shower doors, vinyl shower curtains, counters, sinks and no-wax floors. It also lists where not to use it: wood, painted surfaces, aluminium, fabric, carpet and paper, and it says to rinse it promptly off rubber, plastic and vinyl. That list is the best summary of the bleach question you will find, and it comes from the company that sells the bleach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grout sits in the middle. Sealed, sound grout in a regularly cleaned shower behaves much like tile, and surface mildew on it responds well to a bleach spray. Old, unsealed or crumbling grout is more porous, and black spots that keep returning in the same place within a week or two of cleaning usually mean the growth is deeper than a spray can reach. At that point the fix is to rake out and regrout, or at least reseal once it is clean and dry, rather than reaching for a stronger bottle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will bleach kill black mold?</h3>

          <p>
            Will bleach kill black mold? Black mold is a description, not a species. Plenty of common household molds are dark green or black, and colour does not change how a product works on them. On a non-porous surface, bleach kills dark mold the same way it kills any other. On a porous one, it bleaches the dark colour out of the surface and leaves the colony underneath. The surface decides the answer, not the colour of the spots, and that is true whether the label says black mold, mildew or anything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note from the label: Clorox says the sodium hypochlorite in its bleach products loses strength over time, roughly within a year. An old bottle that has sat under the sink for several years may not be doing much beyond adding a smell. If you are going to use bleach in a bathroom, use a reasonably fresh product, follow the dwell time on the label, rinse, and run the extractor fan while you work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="porous" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Bleach Fails on Wood, Drywall and Other Porous Materials
          </h2>

          <p>
            Porous materials are full of tiny passages that absorb water: the fibres in wood, the paper facing and gypsum core of drywall, the pores of unsealed concrete and block, the backing of carpet. Mold grows into those passages. When you spray a bleach solution onto the face of the material, the explanation remediators usually give is that the chlorine reacts and is used up at or near the surface, where it meets the stain and other organic matter, while the water in the solution keeps soaking in. Household bleach is mostly water, so you are adding moisture to the very material the mold needs moisture to grow in.
          </p>

          <p>
            The visible result is misleading. The surface pigment is oxidised and the stain disappears, so the wall or board looks clean. Underneath, growth that the chlorine did not reach is untouched, and the material is now damper than it was. That combination is why so many people report that mold came back in the same spot after a bleach treatment. It did not really come back. Most of it never left.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bleach also brings its own damage. It can corrode metal fasteners and brackets, weaken some materials with repeated use, and permanently discolour wood and paint. Clorox&#39;s own directions rule out wood and painted surfaces for its Tilex spray for exactly these reasons. Even if bleach did reach every filament inside a stud, you would be trading a mold problem for a bleached, wet and possibly weakened stud.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bleach vs mold on wood</h3>

          <p>
            On structural wood, such as joists, studs, subfloor and roof sheathing, the usual approach is to scrub or wire-brush the surface growth, vacuum it with a HEPA vacuum, apply a registered fungicide that is labelled for porous surfaces and keep it wet for its full contact time, then dry the wood thoroughly. Growth that remains in the grain can be sanded out, and permanent staining can be sealed with a stain-blocking primer. Wood that is soft or crumbling from rot needs replacing, not treating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bleach vs mold on drywall</h3>

          <p>
            Drywall is the worst case for bleach. The paper facing is food for mold, the gypsum core absorbs water, and both are damaged by soaking. EPA guidance on porous materials is blunt: absorbent materials that become moldy, such as ceiling tiles and carpet, may have to be thrown away, because the mold can grow into the empty spaces and crevices and may be impossible to remove completely. Small surface spots on painted drywall can be cleaned and treated, but drywall that is soft, stained through or growing on the back should be cut out and replaced, and the wall cavity dried before new board goes up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="epa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What the EPA Actually Says About Bleach and Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            The EPA&#39;s position is the one most guidance traces back to. In its mold cleanup guidance, including the &quot;Should I use bleach to clean up mold?&quot; page and its mold remediation guide, the EPA says the use of a chemical or biocide that kills organisms such as mold, with chlorine bleach as the example, is not recommended as a routine practice during mold cleanup. It adds that there may be instances when professional judgment indicates its use, for example when immune-compromised people are present.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reasoning matters more than the rule. The EPA points out that it is usually neither possible nor desirable to sterilise an area, because a background level of mold spores will always remain, and those spores will not grow if the moisture problem has been fixed. It also notes that dead mold can still cause allergic reactions in some people, so it is not enough to kill mold. It must be removed. A bleached patch of dead mold still left on a wall has not solved the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            For hard surfaces, the EPA&#39;s basic advice is to scrub mold off with detergent and water and dry the area completely. If you do decide to use a disinfectant or biocide, it says to ventilate the area, exhaust the air to the outdoors, and never mix chlorine bleach with cleaning products that contain ammonia, because toxic fumes could be produced. The CDC&#39;s cleanup advice after floods is consistent: if you use bleach, use no more than 1 cup of household bleach in 1 gallon of water, never use it undiluted, and never mix it with ammonia or other cleaners.
          </p>

          <p>
            OSHA&#39;s archived flood-cleanup guidance takes a similar line for workers, listing bleach as an optional disinfectant only after cleaning with detergent and water, mainly because floodwater can carry sewage. That is a sanitation step for contaminated surfaces, not an endorsement of bleach as the way to kill mold growing inside walls. Across all three agencies the message is the same: clean, dry and fix the moisture first, and treat disinfection as optional and secondary.
          </p>

          <p>
            The EPA also gives a size threshold. If the moldy area is larger than about 10 square feet, roughly a 3-by-3-foot patch, it suggests following its larger-scale remediation guidance, and many homeowners will want a professional at that point. Mold that keeps returning, mold in an HVAC system, or mold after contaminated flooding are also cases where a pro is worth the call. Our guide to <a href="/crawl-space-mold-removal/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">crawl space mold removal</a> covers one of the most common places those larger jobs turn up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Surface by Surface: Bleach or Something Else?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the practical version. For each common surface, the table gives a straight verdict on bleach and what to reach for instead. Product details come from each maker&#39;s label, product page or safety data sheet, the same sources we used in our <a href="/best-mold-remover/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best mold remover guide</a>.
          </p>

