                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No (label says avoid)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Detergent, then RMR-141</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bleach can strip and discolour paint</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drywall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">RMR-141 on small surface spots</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace if soft or stained through</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, block</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scrub, then RMR-141</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fix seepage; consider a wall sealer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carpet, ceiling tile</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually replace</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">EPA: absorbent materials may need discarding</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leftover stains (after killing)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Removes stain only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">RMR-86, on bleach-safe surfaces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">RMR-86 itself does not kill mold</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. First, bleach is only the right answer in the top rows, where the material is smooth and sealed. Second, on almost every porous surface the recommendation is the same product, RMR-141, because it is an EPA-registered fungicide whose maker lists porous as well as non-porous surfaces, and it does not whiten or corrode the way bleach does. Its registration number is 61178-2-93443, and it uses two quaternary ammonium compounds at 0.07 percent each rather than chlorine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete and block deserve a word. Bleach will lighten surface mold on a basement wall, but unsealed masonry is porous and the mold is usually there because water is wicking through it. Clean and treat the growth, then deal with the water: grading, gutters, a <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">basement wall sealer</a> for minor seepage, and humidity control for the air. Otherwise the wall will grow mold again whatever you sprayed on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Bleach Safety: The Mistakes That Hurt People
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you do use bleach, the biggest danger is not the mold. It is what bleach does when it meets other cleaners. Mixing bleach with ammonia, which is found in some glass cleaners and multi-surface sprays, produces chloramine gas. Mixing it with acids, which include vinegar, many toilet bowl cleaners and rust removers, releases chlorine gas. Both can injure your lungs in a small, closed bathroom. Clorox&#39;s own label warns against mixing its product with toilet bowl cleaners, rust removers, acids or ammonia.
          </p>

          <p>
            This matters especially for mold, because people often try one product, see the stain return, and reach for another. Someone who sprays vinegar on a patch of mildew and follows it with bleach an hour later can create exactly the reaction the label warns about. If you switch products, rinse the surface with plain water and let it dry first. Our guide to whether <a href="/does-vinegar-kill-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">vinegar kills mold</a> explains where vinegar fits and why the two must never meet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilation is the next rule. Open a window, run the bathroom fan, and take breaks if the smell builds up. The EPA says to exhaust the air to the outdoors whenever you use a disinfectant or biocide. Wear rubber or nitrile gloves and eye protection, because a splash of bleach in the eye is a trip to urgent care, and protect clothing and nearby fabric. For stain removers like RMR-86, which the safety data sheet lists at 1 to 5 percent sodium hypochlorite, RMR recommends a respirator with appropriate cartridges, protective clothing and nitrile gloves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not use bleach undiluted on mold in the house, and do not use more than the label calls for. Stronger does not mean better on a porous surface, because the problem is reach, not strength. If you are dealing with a large area, mold you can smell but not find, or someone in the house with asthma or a weakened immune system, the EPA&#39;s advice to consider a professional applies before you open any bottle.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER MIX</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Bleach plus ammonia, or bleach plus vinegar or other acids, makes toxic gas.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Rinse and dry between products. Ventilate. Wear gloves and eye protection. Never use bleach undiluted on household mold.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Use Instead, and How to Keep Mold From Coming Back
          </h2>

          <p>
            For shower grout, tile and tubs, keep using a bleach spray if you like it. Clorox Plus Tilex is the right tool there and the one most people already own. Spray, let it work, rinse, and dry the surfaces afterwards with a squeegee or towel. If you would rather avoid chlorine fumes in a small bathroom, a registered non-bleach fungicide also works on those surfaces, it just will not remove staining as quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For wood, drywall, concrete and other porous surfaces, use a registered fungicide labelled for them. RMR-141 is our pick. The label directions for mold are to saturate the surface and keep it wet for 10 minutes, then wipe or let it air dry. That 10-minute wet contact is what the kill claim depends on, so on a vertical wall you may need to re-spray to keep it wet. Spraying and wiping immediately does not deliver what the label promises. Our <a href="/rmr-141-review/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">RMR-141 review</a> goes through the label, the safety data sheet and where it fits in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then deal with the stain separately, if it bothers you. Killing mold does not remove the dark marks it leaves, and RMR-141 does not remove stains. RMR-86 is the stain step. It is worth knowing why it works so fast: RMR-86 is itself bleach-based, and RMR&#39;s own FAQ says it does not kill mold, it only removes the stains. That is the same split this whole page describes. Kill with a registered fungicide, then whiten with bleach if you need to, on surfaces bleach will not damage. On wood or drywall where you do not want bleach, sand or seal the stain with a stain-blocking primer instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The real fix is moisture</h3>

          <p>
            None of these products stops mold returning if the surface stays damp. EPA guidance is to dry water-damaged materials within 24 to 48 hours to prevent mold growth, and to keep indoor relative humidity below 60 percent, ideally between 30 and 50 percent. Fix leaks, vent bathrooms and dryers outdoors, and watch for condensation on cold walls and windows. A cheap hygrometer tells you quickly whether a room is in range.
          </p>

          <p>
            In basements, laundry rooms and other spaces that sit above 60 percent for weeks at a time, a dehumidifier is the tool that actually keeps mold away. For most rooms a 50-pint unit is the sensible size, and our guide to the <a href="/best-50-pint-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best 50-pint dehumidifier</a> covers the models worth buying, including Wi-Fi units that let you set the target humidity and check it from your phone. For larger or wetter basements, see our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">basement dehumidifier guide</a>. If the room already stays between 30 and 50 percent, you do not need one, and money is better spent on fixing the leak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put together, the routine is simple. Find and fix the water. Remove what is too far gone. Scrub what can be saved, treat porous surfaces with RMR-141 for the full 10 minutes, use bleach only on hard bathroom surfaces, deal with stains last, and keep the humidity down so the problem does not start again. For the full product comparison, see our <a href="/best-mold-remover/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best mold remover</a> picks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging success by the stain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A stain that disappears in seconds proves the bleach reached the pigment, not that the colony is dead. On porous materials growth can remain inside the material. Judge the job by whether mold returns over the next few weeks, and by whether you have fixed the moisture that let it grow in the first place.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying bleach on drywall or wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clorox itself says not to use its Tilex spray on wood or painted surfaces. On drywall and bare wood bleach adds water to the material, discolours it and does not reliably reach growth inside. Use a registered fungicide labelled for porous surfaces, and replace drywall that is soft or stained through.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Following vinegar with bleach</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mixing bleach with acids such as vinegar releases chlorine gas, and mixing it with ammonia releases chloramine. Both can happen on the surface if you switch products without rinsing. Rinse with plain water and let the area dry between products, ventilate the room, and never combine cleaners in a bucket.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the contact time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A fungicide kill claim depends on the surface staying wet for the time on the label. RMR-141 needs 10 minutes of wet contact for mold. Spraying and wiping straight away, or letting a vertical wall dry in two minutes, means the label claim does not apply. Re-spray to keep it wet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating mold but not the moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every product on this page is a second line of defence. Mold returns wherever a surface stays damp, so fix leaks, dry wet materials within 24 to 48 hours as EPA guidance advises, and keep humidity below 60 percent, ideally 30 to 50, with ventilation or a dehumidifier.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does bleach kill mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On hard, non-porous surfaces such as glazed tile, glass, tubs and sealed counters, yes. Bleach kills surface mold and removes the stain. On porous materials such as wood, drywall and concrete it mostly whitens the stain while growth inside the material remains. The EPA does not recommend bleach as a routine part of mold cleanup; detergent, water and drying come first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will bleach kill mold on wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. Wood is porous, so mold grows into the grain where a bleach spray does not dependably reach, and the water in the solution soaks in. Bleach also discolours wood, and Clorox says not to use its Tilex spray on wood. Scrub, apply a registered fungicide such as RMR-141 for its full 10-minute contact time, then dry it completely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will bleach kill black mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Black mold is a colour, not a separate kind of mold that responds differently. On non-porous surfaces bleach kills dark mold as well as any other. On porous ones it bleaches the black colour out and leaves growth underneath. If black mold covers more than about 10 square feet, EPA guidance suggests a larger-scale approach, often a professional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does bleach kill black mold on drywall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, not dependably. Drywall paper and gypsum absorb water and mold grows into them, so bleach removes the surface stain without reaching growth inside, and the soaking can damage the board. Small surface spots on painted drywall can be cleaned and treated with a registered fungicide. Drywall that is soft or stained through should be cut out and replaced.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does mold come back after bleach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because it never left. Bleach removed the visible stain while the colony inside a porous material survived, or the moisture that fed it was never fixed. Mold also returns on any surface that stays damp. Fix the water source, treat porous surfaces with a registered fungicide, and keep indoor humidity between 30 and 50 percent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kills mold better than bleach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On porous surfaces, an EPA-registered fungicide labelled for them, such as RMR-141, a quaternary ammonium product that requires 10 minutes of wet contact for mold and does not whiten. On shower tile and grout, a bleach spray like Clorox Plus Tilex works well. Vinegar is not a registered mold killer. Whatever you use, removing the mold and fixing moisture matter most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to mix bleach with other cleaners for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Bleach with ammonia produces chloramine gas, and bleach with acids such as vinegar, toilet bowl cleaner or rust remover releases chlorine gas. Use one product at a time, rinse with water and let the surface dry before switching, ventilate the room, and wear gloves and eye protection. The CDC says never to use bleach undiluted.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

