        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Bleach kills mold where mold cannot hide: glazed tile, glass, tubs and sealed surfaces. Keep it for the shower. On wood, drywall, concrete and anything else that soaks up water, it removes the stain and leaves the problem, which is why the EPA does not recommend it as a routine part of mold cleanup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For porous surfaces, use a registered fungicide such as RMR-141 and keep it wet for the full 10 minutes, then remove stains separately if you need to. And fix the moisture, because a room held between 30 and 50 percent humidity is the only mold treatment that keeps working after you put the bottle away.
          </p>
